    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d300">

                <pb facs="0469" n="389" xml:id="pg389"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Mutual Guarantee (Balkan)/164:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1937-01-16T13:00:00+00:00">January 16, 1937—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 16—11:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>68. For the Secretary and Moore. I questioned Léger this morning with regard to
                    the project reported to the Department in my telegram 1273, December 18, 1 p.m.,
                    1936.</p>
                <p>Léger said that the French Government had offered to enter into pacts of mutual
                    assistance with Yugoslavia and Rumania (as well as one with Czechoslovakia)
                    provided Yugoslavia, Rumania and Czechoslovakia should alter their present
                    obligations for defense against Hungary and Bulgaria into a general pact of
                    mutual assistance against attack from any source.</p>
                <p>Léger said that no progress had been made because Yugoslavia was not anxious to
                    disturb her present relations with Germany and Italy and feared German and
                    Italian resentment. He said that Czechoslovakia was most anxious to conclude
                    such a pact and he believed that Rumania would agree provided the objections of
                    Yugoslavia could be overcome.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
